{"data":{"id":59956,"api_model":"artworks","api_link":"https:\/\/api.artic.edu\/api\/v1\/artworks\/59956","is_boosted":false,"title":"The Wedding of Peleus and Thetis","alt_titles":[],"thumbnail":{"lqip":"data:image\/gif;base64,","width":7698,"height":4832,"alt_text":"A work made of oil on panel."},"main_reference_number":"1947.108","has_not_been_viewed_much":false,"boost_rank":null,"date_start":1636,"date_end":1636,"date_display":"1636","date_qualifier_title":null,"date_qualifier_id":null,"artist_display":"Peter Paul Rubens (Flemish, 1577\u20131640)","place_of_origin":"Flanders","description":"<p>In this oil sketch, the Flemish artist Peter Paul Rubens illustrated a moment from the ancient Roman poem <em>Metamorphoses<\/em> for the hunting lodge of King Philip IV of Spain. In the middle of a wedding celebration, the goddess of discord, Eris, has thrown a golden apple into the feast to provoke jealousy. She succeeds in igniting a competition for the extraordinary fruit between Venus, goddess of beauty, who sits nude in the foreground; Juno, goddess of marriage, who wears a flowing veil at the center; and Minerva, goddess of war, who stands at the left in a helmet. Rubens humanized the gods through robust modeling of their bodies and facial expressions, a choice appropriate to the subject matter, a story of their pride and vanity.<\/p>\n","short_description":null,"dimensions":"27 \u00d7 42.6 cm (10 5\/8 \u00d7 16 3\/4 in.); Framed: 42.2 \u00d7 57.8 \u00d7 6.4 cm (16 5\/8 \u00d7 22 3\/4 \u00d7 2 1\/2 in.)","dimensions_detail":[{"depth":null,"width":42,"height":27,"diameter":null,"clarification":null},{"depth":6,"width":57,"height":42,"diameter":null,"clarification":"Framed"}],"medium_display":"Oil on panel","inscriptions":null,"credit_line":"Charles H. and Mary F. S. Worcester Collection","catalogue_display":null,"publication_history":null,"exhibition_history":null,"provenance_text":null,"edition":null,"publishing_verification_level":"Web Basic","internal_department_i